# Intent: A clock face (features.md, Faces and look: big digits or ring, dimmed at night, works with no host once the time is set). maipai_face_clock_core.h is plain C: the four digits of a 12 or 24 hour clock (12 at midnight and noon, no leading zero), seven-bar digit shapes and their rectangles (no large font is added; the digits are drawn from bars and scale cleanly), the two layouts (big digits, or smaller digits inside a seconds ring), the date line in English (Sun Nov 15) and Spanish (dom 15 nov; plain ASCII, the screen font has no accents yet), and the rule for when the clock shows: only when the time is set, the unit is idle and nothing blocks it, because the host asked (self.face.show_clock, 1 to 3600 s, 0 goes back) or because the unit was idle for clock_idle_s (0 by default: never by itself). The clock never covers a mute, vision-off or quiet look or a ringing timer, dims to 55 percent at night, and a touch ends it: that touch is only a wake (it starts no listen and toggles no privacy state). The bars and labels exist only while the clock shows. Behind CONFIG_MAIPAI_FACE_CLOCK (default y); off, none of it is compiled.
# Placement: unit (the clock runs on the robot with the host off)
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/board/stackchan_display.cc of the StackChan tree (MIT); new file hal/maipai_face_clock_core.h
# Check after a flash: self.face.show_clock {"seconds":20}: the clock fills the screen with the right time, the colon blinks, the date line is in the chosen language; a touch returns the face and does not start a listen; with clock_idle_s 60 the clock comes up after a quiet minute on its own; while muted it does not show and the tool says why.
